- Retrieved practices:
- Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) Input Validation and Allowlisting
WHAT: User-controlled URLs, IPs, or domains are passed directly to server-side request APIs, allowing attackers to make the application send arbitrary internal/external network requests (SSRF).
WHY: This can expose internal services, bypass network controls, and enable data exfiltration or further injection attacks.
HOW: Treat all user-supplied endpoints as untrusted; parse and validate them with robust libraries, then enforce an allowlist of known-good hosts/URLs or service identifiers before issuing any outbound request.
EXAMPLE: Before: `requests.get(user_input_url)`; After: `if user_input_url in ALLOWED_ENDPOINTS: requests.get(user_input_url)`

- Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) Prevention
WHAT: Prevent making server-side HTTP/HTTPS or other protocol requests to attacker-controlled URLs, especially when the URL or its components come from user input.
WHY: Unvalidated outbound requests can let attackers access internal services, metadata endpoints, or perform port scans from your server.
HOW: Validate and sanitize all user-controlled request targets; enforce an allowlist of protocols and domains; use IPAddress.TryParse() and Uri.CheckHostName() to confirm valid hosts; disable following HTTP redirects; never forward raw HTTP responses directly to users.
EXAMPLE (C# before): `var resp = await http.GetAsync(userUrl); return await resp.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();` → (after): `if (!IsAllowed(userUrl)) return BadRequest(); var resp = await http.GetAsync(allowedUrl, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead); return MapToSafeDto(resp);`

- Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via HTML/Script Injection
WHAT: User-controlled input is inserted into HTML, attributes, URLs, CSS, XML, or SSI/PHP contexts without proper encoding or filtering, allowing attackers to inject script or HTML that executes in the victim’s browser (e.g., via DIV styles, BASE/OBJECT/IMG tags, XML islands, SSI/PHP, or renamed .js files).
WHY: Malicious scripts can run with the user’s privileges, enabling credential theft, session hijacking, unauthorized actions (e.g., admin commands via IMG requests), data exfiltration, or malware delivery.
HOW: Treat all user input as untrusted; for each output context (HTML body, attribute, URL, CSS, JS, XML, SSI, etc.) apply strict allowlists and context-appropriate escaping/encoding; disable or strip dangerous constructs (e.g., `javascript:`, `expression`, event handlers, `OBJECT/APPLET/EMBED` where not needed); avoid reflecting raw input into SSI/PHP or file paths; use a vetted XSS protection library or framework output-encoding APIs.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe, HTML context): `echo "<div>" . $_GET['name'] . "</div>";` After (PHP with HTML encoding): `echo "<div>" . htmlspecialchars($_GET['name'], ENT_QUOTES | ENT_SUBSTITUTE, 'UTF-8') . "</div>";`

- DOM-based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention in JavaScript
WHAT: DOM-based XSS occurs when untrusted data is treated as HTML, code, or markup in the browser (e.g., using innerHTML, outerHTML, eval, or event-handler attributes) instead of as plain text.
WHY: Attackers can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript in the victim’s browser, leading to session theft, data exfiltration, UI redress, or full account compromise.
HOW: Treat untrusted data only as displayable text; avoid eval and APIs that interpret strings as code/HTML; prefer textContent/innerText and safe DOM APIs (createElement, setAttribute for non-event attributes, appendChild); JavaScript-encode and quote untrusted data when building JS templates; never place untrusted data into event-handler attributes or HTML-rendering sinks.
EXAMPLE: Vulnerable: `div.innerHTML = location.hash;` and `eval(userInput);`
Secure: `div.textContent = location.hash;` and avoid eval by using explicit logic or safe parsing of expected data types.



